WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.890
With the release of Shazam!, Warner Bros.
has another superhero hit on its hands, further

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:09.090
proving that you don't have to make your movie
part of the MCU to have a good time.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:13.510
But while the movie's ending is pretty straightforward,
there are enough head-scratching and surprising

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:17.410
moments in the movie's latter third that might
be in need of some explanation.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:18.410
Don't sweat it!

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:19.410
That's why we're here.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:23.320
Let's say the magic words — spoilers ahead!
— and explain the ending of Shazam!

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:27.280
If you're not a longtime comic book reader,
you may have scratched your head at some of

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:31.090
the superhero names Billy and Freddie came
up with over the course of the film.

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:34.520
From Thundercrack to the Red Cyclone, none
of them seemed to stick.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.600
By the end of the film, the big dude in red
played by Zachary Levi never seemed to actually

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.650
get the kind of codename that gets attached
to other superheroes.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:45.780
You'd think after so many attempts at getting
him a codename, he'd do better than "Shazam,"

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.560
a name he can never say without losing his
powers in a bolt of lightning.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.120
SHAZAM!

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:53.400
So what's the deal?

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.620
The short version is that the superhero we
meet in Shazam! didn't always go by this name...in

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:02.860
fact, he used to go by Captain Marvel, back
when he first debuted in 1939.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:05.160
"Shazam!"

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.000
"Billy becomes Captain Marvel!"

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:13.060
But years later, while Captain Marvel comics
weren't being published, Timely Comics changed

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:16.170
its name to the more familiar Marvel Comics
we all know and love.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:21.459
After Marvel created its own Captain Marvel
character in 1967, DC and Marvel Comics had

00:01:21.459 --> 00:01:24.380
to come to an agreement about how to navigate
the tricky trademarks.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:29.920
Eventually, in 2011 DC just gave up "Captain
Marvel" entirely, and simply renamed the character

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:30.930
"Shazam."

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:34.080
So if you were wondering why he never settled
on the pretty good name of...

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:35.080
"Captain Sparklefingers!"

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:36.170
"No, it’s not.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:37.849
That's not my name!"

00:01:37.849 --> 00:01:39.440
...well, now you know why.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:44.200
The final battle with Doctor Sivana and the
Seven Deadly Sins provided one of the most

00:01:44.209 --> 00:01:48.859
awesome climactic moments in recent superhero
movie history: when Shazam extends his powers

00:01:48.859 --> 00:01:53.099
to his foster siblings and creates five more
superheroes to save the day.

00:01:53.099 --> 00:01:57.419
Now instead of one new superhero joining the
DCEU, there are actually six.

00:01:57.419 --> 00:02:01.749
If you wondered how and why the movie could
justify pulling five super-rabbits out of

00:02:01.749 --> 00:02:05.310
its magical hat, there's actually a pretty
nice set-up for this final act twist earlier

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.509
in the film that you might've missed if you
weren't paying close attention.

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:12.500
When Billy encounters the wizard Shazam, the
old man yells about the empty chairs behind

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:16.170
him on the Rock of Eternity — chairs that
once belonged to his brothers and sisters

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.500
in magic, who have all long-since died.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.340
When Billy shares his powers, he's filling
five more of those seats...though just who

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:26.310
will wind up sitting in that seventh chair
is a question we probably won't have answered

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:27.600
until the next film.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.940
However, since the film seemed to pretty closely
follow the plot of DC's New 52 Shazam! miniseries,

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.600
there's a good bet that the current run of
Shazam! comics DC is publishing will offer

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.030
some clues as to the seventh Shazam's identity.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.450
...or, it could be Hoppy, the Marvel Bunny!!

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:42.600
Okay, probably not.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.520
But speaking of hidden identities...

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:49.080
The very last scene before the credits rolled
offered up a really fun moment — when Billy's

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.760
special guest Superman makes an appearance
of his own.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.950
Of course, while the audience saw Superman's
blue supersuit, the shot was conspicuously

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.570
composed to keep the actor's head out of frame.

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:02.790
Considering that Superman is one of the most
popular and important superheroes ever created,

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:06.581
it might've seemed weird to the average moviegoer
that the filmmakers went out of their way

00:03:06.581 --> 00:03:09.250
to not show their most bankable hero's face.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:13.380
The answer is that there's some drama going
on behind the scenes at Warner Bros. and DC

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:17.030
Films regarding just who is going to wear
the big blue suit going forward.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:21.250
The most recent actor to take the role of
Superman, Henry Cavill, is reportedly in talks

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:25.620
with Warner Bros. about whether or not he'll
return to play the character in future movies.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:30.030
But while Cavill's future as Superman is murky,
the filmmakers still wanted to have a Superman

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:34.780
cameo in their film, so they did the next-best-thing:
they hired a buff dude to put on the suit,

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.860
while simply keeping his face out of frame.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:41.310
The moment in the film lands as pretty funny
even if you don't know about Cavill's alleged

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:44.130
contract disputes, and it's even funnier if
you do.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:48.600
And hey, it also meant they didn't have to
spend their CGI budget on Cavill's mustache,

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.080
so it's a win-win.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:54.680
One of the reasons Shazam! is so much fun
is that it's an exceptionally weird movie.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:59.010
It's got a magic wizard who lives in a cave,
the physical embodiments of religious concepts,

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:00.959
and an unexplained obsession with tigers.

00:04:00.959 --> 00:04:05.319
But weirdest of all, it's got a talking, evil
worm, who shows up in the film's mid-credits

00:04:05.319 --> 00:04:09.010
scene in Doctor Sivana's prison cell, ranting
about Seven Realms.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:13.250
As with so many elements of superhero movies,
there was probably a sizeable portion of the

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:15.660
audience that saw this and couldn't be more
confused.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:20.630
Don't feel bad: this little guy is pretty
bizarre and obscure, even for comic book fans.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:22.069
Let's just dig into this thing:

00:04:22.069 --> 00:04:25.370
The little talking bug is a longtime Shazam
villain named Mister Mind.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:28.400
He's a talking, super-intelligent worm from
another planet.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:32.300
That planet is sometimes Venus, depending
on which comic books you're reading.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:36.469
He's the founder of a team of supervillains
in the comics called the Monster Society of

00:04:36.469 --> 00:04:41.139
Evil, which has included members with names
like Jeepers and Mr. Banjo, just to name a

00:04:41.139 --> 00:04:42.139
few.

00:04:42.139 --> 00:04:43.150
It's...it's pretty wild.

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:46.840
As for the Seven Realms, this seems like it
refers to a pretty recent addition to the

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:49.250
pages of DC Comics' Shazam! series.

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:53.479
As of the making of this video, the comics
are only starting to dig into what these realms

00:04:53.479 --> 00:04:56.720
are, or their significance, so if you want
to know more about what's likely to happen

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:59.400
in a sequel to Shazam!, start reading.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:04.240
Shazam! not only seems to have plenty of potential
for sequels starring Billy Batson, Freddie

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.910
Freeman, and the rest of the Shazam Family,
but it also easily connects to the wider DC

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:10.050
Extended Universe.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:11.190
"I'm Batman!"

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:12.340
"Get'im Batman!"

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:16.990
Aquaman from 2018 proved that DC movies don't
have to be grim and brooding.

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:21.740
But 2019's Shazam! took things to the next
level and showed that DC Comics movies can

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:26.279
have strong connections to each other without
being bogged down in cinematic universe set-up

00:05:26.279 --> 00:05:27.860
and continuity gobbledygook.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:32.319
Meanwhile, there's tons of set-up for future
interactions with other DC heroes.

00:05:32.319 --> 00:05:36.740
We already caught a partial glimpse of Shazam
teaming up with Superman in the Fawcett Central

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:37.740
lunchroom.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:40.340
The second post-credits scene hints at another
possible team up:

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:41.620
"I can't talk to fish!

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:43.270
What can we do with that that's cool?"

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:46.800
"I don't know, maybe command an army of billions
in the ocean?"

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:47.920
"Oh yeah."

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:51.509
While Warner Bros. undoubtedly stumbled out
of the gate with their early efforts in terms

00:05:51.509 --> 00:05:56.080
of overall critical reception and box office
performance, Aquaman and Shazam! seem to have

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:57.550
fully righted the ship.

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:01.860
It remains to be seen whether or not the forthcoming
slate of DC movies will actually have any

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.279
connections to this film.

00:06:03.279 --> 00:06:07.289
But if Warner Bros. can find a way to take
the lessons of Shazam! and make Superman,

00:06:07.289 --> 00:06:12.860
Batman, and the rest of the gang fun again...well,
we'll be in for a pretty super batch of movies.

